<article class="env-remark" id="069A">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/069A">Remark <span data-tag="069A">1.5.4.11</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a category and let $S$ be a collection of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Then: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
For every object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the identity morphism $\operatorname{id}_{X}$ is a transfinite composition of morphisms of $S$. This follows by taking $\alpha = 0$ in Definition <a data-tag="006M" href="/tag/006M">1.5.4.10</a>. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Every morphism $f: X \rightarrow Y$ which belongs to $S$ is a transfinite composition of morphisms of $S$. This follows by taking $\alpha =1$ in Definition <a data-tag="006M" href="/tag/006M">1.5.4.10</a>. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $f: X \rightarrow Y$ and $g: Y \rightarrow Z$ are morphisms of $S$, then the composition $g \circ f$ is a transfinite composition of morphisms of $S$. This follows by taking $\alpha = 2$ in Definition <a data-tag="006M" href="/tag/006M">1.5.4.10</a>. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
<p> In particular, if $S$ is closed under transfinite composition, then it is closed under composition and contains all identity morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. </p>
</article>
